## DESCRIPTION
## Find the intersect of two planes written in parametric form.
## ENDDESCRIPTION

## DBsubject(Linear algebra)
## DBchapter(Inner products)
## DBsection(Projection and distance)
## Date(September 5, 2011)
## Institution(Fitchburg State University)
## Author(Peter Staab)
## KEYWORDS('lines')

# updated 2022.12.12

DOCUMENT();    # This should be the first executable line in the problem.
loadMacros(
    'PGstandard.pl',        'PGML.pl',
    'parserVectorUtils.pl', 'parserParametricLine.pl',
    'PGcourse.pl'
);

Context('Vector');
Context()->variables->are(t => 'Real');

do {
    $v1  = ColumnVector(non_zero_vector3D(-5, 5));
    @vv1 = $v1->value;
    $v2  = ColumnVector(non_zero_vector3D(-5, 5));
    @vv2 = $v2->value;
    $v3  = ColumnVector(non_zero_vector3D(-5, 5));
    @vv3 = $v3->value;
    $v4  = ColumnVector(non_zero_vector3D(-5, 5));
    @vv4 = $v4->value;
    $v5  = ColumnVector(non_zero_vector3D(-5, 5));
    @vv5 = $v5->value;

    $A = Matrix([
        [ $vv1[0], $vv2[0], -$vv4[0], -$vv5[0], $vv3[0] ],
        [ $vv1[1], $vv2[1], -$vv4[1], -$vv5[1], $vv3[1] ],
        [ $vv1[2], $vv2[2], -$vv4[2], -$vv5[2], $vv3[2] ]
    ]);

    $A0 = Matrix([
        [ $vv1[0], $vv2[0], -$vv4[0] ],
        [ $vv1[1], $vv2[1], -$vv4[1] ],
        [ $vv1[2], $vv2[2], -$vv4[2] ]
    ]);
    $d = $A0->det;

} until ($d != 0 && abs($d) <= 5);

$A_rr = $A0->inverse * $A;
$w    = ColumnVector($A_rr->column(4));
$P    = ColumnVector($A_rr->column(5));

$ans1 = ParametricLine("$P+t*$w");

BEGIN_PGML
Find the intersection of the planes, [`P_1`] and [`P_2`] where

[```
\begin{aligned}
P_1 & =  \left\{ [$v1] t + [$v2] s \; | \; t, s \in \mathbb{R} \right\} \\
P_2 & =  \left\{ [$v3]  + [$v4] p + [$v5] q \; | \; p, q \in \mathbb{R} \right\}
\end{aligned}
```]

[` P_1 \bigcap P_2 `] = [____]{$ans1}

Write your answer in the vector form [` \vec{P} + t \vec{w} `] like [|<1,2,3>+t<4,5,6>|]

END_PGML

BEGIN_PGML_SOLUTION
Overall, the intersection between two planes is a line and this problem seeks
a line in the form of [`\vec{P} + t \vec{w}.`]

There are a number of ways to solve this.  We will set the two planes equal
to each other or
[``` [$v1] t + [$v2] s  = [$v3]  + [$v4] p + [$v5] q ```]
or
[``` [$v1] t + [$v2] s - [$v4] p - [$v5] q = [$v3] ```]

Which can be written as the augmented coefficent matrix

[```[$A]```]
and row reducing results in

[```[$A_rr]```]

And the 4th column will be the vector [`\vec{w}`] and vector [`\vec{P}`] is
the last column. Thus the solution is
[```[$w] t + [$P]```]
END_PGML_SOLUTION

ENDDOCUMENT();    # This should be the last executable line in the problem.
